        "title": "Russia's boreal forests and wild grasses could combat climate change",
        "text": "A  Scientists believe that Russia's ancient forests are the country's best natural weapon against climate change, even though the store of carbon beneath the ground also makes these areas vulnerable to carbon release. A recent study found that half the world's carbon is stored in land in the permafrost region, about two-thirds of which lies in Russia. On top of former glaciers grows a mix of conifers called the boreal forest. \"There's a lot of carbon there and it's very vulnerable,\" says Josep Canadell, co-author of the study. \"If the permafrost thaws, we could be releasing ten per cent more carbon a year for several centuries, more than our previous models predicted. It's going to cost a lot to reduce our emissions by that much, but it will cost more in damage if we don't.\"\n\nB  The study was published in Global Biogeochemical Cycles. The researchers found that the region contains 1,672 billion tons of organic carbon, much of it several feet underground, which \"would account for approximately 50 per cent of the estimated global below-ground organic carbon\". Another paper, published in Nature, found that old forests, which make up perhaps half of the boreal forest, \"continue to accumulate carbon, contrary to the long-standing view that they are carbon-neutral\". Although fires and insect infestations destroy whole areas of forest and release the carbon they contain into the atmosphere, old-growth forests still take in more than these natural disturbances release, says the lead author, Sebastiaan Luyssaert, a biologist at the University of Antwerp in Belgium. \"This is all the more reason to protect Russia's boreal forests,\" which take in 500 million tons of carbon a year, or about one-fifth of the carbon absorbed by the world's land, says Mr Canadell, who is executive director of the Global Carbon Project, based in Canberra.\n\nC  Jing Ming Chen, a geography professor at the University of Toronto who specialises in climate modelling for the boreal region, says: \"Cutting down boreal trees increases the amount of carbon in the atmosphere, and it takes 50 to 100 years to put that carbon back in the ground.\" Luyssaert and Chen argue that there is a strong case for conserving the old-growth forests. \"It's better to keep as much carbon in the forest as possible right now,\" Mr Luyssaert explains. \"If we want to avoid irreversible processes like melting permafrost or changing ocean currents, we absolutely have to control our emissions in the next two or three decades. It's a case where you need to be short-sighted to be far-sighted.\" \"The threats to the boreal forests don't seem significant right now,\" explains Nigel Roulet, a carbon cycle specialist at McGill University in Montreal, \"but I'm convinced pressure will increase as the region gets warmer and it becomes easier to operate there. I also expect these resources to become more valuable as others are exhausted.\"\n\nD  Scientists say Russia and Kazakhstan could make a unique contribution to the fight against global warming by harvesting the wild grasses that have overgrown 100,000 square miles of farmland abandoned in the 1990s, and using them to make ethanol, or, better still, burning them in coal-fuelled power stations. According to Nicolas Vuichard, principal author of a paper published in Environmental Science and Technology, of Washington, DC, using the grasses to make ethanol would lock away in the ground, over 60 years, about 10 million tons of carbon a year: a quarter as dead root matter in the soil, and the rest by producing ethanol to replace petrol-based fuels. \"That's not huge on a world scale, but it's substantial,\" he says. Fossil fuels emit about eight billion tons of carbon a year, of which about two billion tons are absorbed by plants and soil. Renton Righelato, visiting research fellow at the University of Reading and former chairman of the World Land Trust, agrees. \"Given that it would take the world's entire supply of arable land to replace just two-thirds of our transport fuel needs,\" he says, \"biofuels are not a practical long-term solution for transport emissions. What we need is carbon-free fuel. But in the case of abandoned croplands, using grasses as biofuels could make a contribution.\" A co-author of the study, Adam Wolf, of the Carnegie Institution for Science at Stanford University, cites a study by Elliott Campbell in the journal Science which showed that burning grasses in a coal-fuelled power station doubles the savings in carbon emissions, compared with using the same grasses to make ethanol. \"If biofuels are going to reduce emissions, using abandoned croplands to make electricity and replace coal is our best bet,\" he says. \"Both of these countries have coal-fuelled power stations, so the process could start soon.\" Russia and Kazakhstan are therefore now in a position to become leaders in green energy, and could use the grasses to export clean electricity as well as oil and gas, according to Mr Wolf."
